Relevant columns for this task  
Movie.year : contains the release year for every film – basis for all time–period calculations.  
Movie.MID / Movie.title : could be used for counting or checking duplicates when grouping.  
(Every other table is irrelevant here, because the question only needs “how many movies were released in a 10‑year window”.)

Possible conditions  
• Treat “year” as an integer → CAST(year AS INTEGER).  
• Ten‑year window is inclusive of both ends:  start_year  ≤  year  ≤  start_year+9.  
• We need one window for every distinct start_year that exists in the data.

Below are ten exploratory SQL queries (LIMIT 20 rows each) that go from very simple inspection to the final answer.  
(Replace … with your real database/connection runner.)

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a few raw rows to confirm column content
SELECT * FROM "Movie" LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. List DISTINCT years as stored (string form)
SELECT DISTINCT "year" 
FROM "Movie"
ORDER BY "year" 
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Same list but cast to INTEGER, so we can do math
SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS start_year
FROM "Movie"
ORDER BY start_year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Count how many movies in each single calendar year
SELECT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS year_int,
       COUNT(*) AS movie_cnt
FROM "Movie"
GROUP BY year_int
ORDER BY year_int
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Small experiment: how many movies came out between 2000 and 2009?
SELECT COUNT(*) AS movies_2000s
FROM "Movie"
WHERE CAST("year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN 2000 AND 2009;
```

```sql
-- 6. Build one 10‑year window for EVERY distinct start_year
SELECT  s.start_year,
        (SELECT COUNT(*) 
         FROM "Movie" m
         WHERE CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN s.start_year AND s.start_year+9
        ) AS movies_in_window
FROM (SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS start_year
      FROM "Movie") AS s
ORDER BY s.start_year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Same as #6 but sort by descending count so we see the densest decades first
SELECT  s.start_year,
        (SELECT COUNT(*) 
         FROM "Movie" m
         WHERE CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN s.start_year AND s.start_year+9
        ) AS movies_in_window
FROM (SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS start_year
      FROM "Movie") AS s
ORDER BY movies_in_window DESC, s.start_year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Add a tie‑breaker: earliest start_year wins if counts equal
SELECT  s.start_year,
        (SELECT COUNT(*) 
         FROM "Movie" m
         WHERE CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN s.start_year AND s.start_year+9
        ) AS movies_in_window
FROM (SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS start_year
      FROM "Movie") AS s
ORDER BY movies_in_window DESC, s.start_year ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Verify the maximum number of movies among all windows
SELECT MAX(window_cnt) AS max_decade_total
FROM (
    SELECT  (SELECT COUNT(*) 
             FROM "Movie" m
             WHERE CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN s.start_year AND s.start_year+9
            ) AS window_cnt
    FROM (SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS start_year
          FROM "Movie") AS s
);
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER: return only the start year & count for the single best 10‑year period
SELECT  s.start_year,
        (SELECT COUNT(*) 
         FROM "Movie" m
         WHERE CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN s.start_year AND s.start_year+9
        ) AS movies_in_window
FROM (SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS start_year
      FROM "Movie") AS s
ORDER BY movies_in_window DESC, s.start_year ASC
LIMIT 1;
```

Running query #10 will output something like:

start_year | movies_in_window  
-----------|------------------  
2010       | 123             

